WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.419 --> 00:00:03.299
Welcome to this video.

2
00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:05.400
This is the second video on examples.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.459
In this case, we are going to be

4
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:11.460
querying to layer data within a landscape.

5
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.699
Just a quick example of how you can use that to generate your PCG.

6
00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:22.000
Here we are, and we are on slide 2,

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.239
example 2, painting PCGs.

8
00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:27.320
In this, we're going to see a very simple,

9
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.320
straightforward square of a landscape.

10
00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:35.000
What's interesting about this landscape is,

11
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.840
it's creating the grass,

12
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.439
but it's only creating the grass where

13
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.880
we are using a landscape layer of grass.

14
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.799
To demonstrate this, if I was to come into

15
00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:51.520
the selection mode for landscape,

16
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.240
let's expand this out a little bit.

17
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.439
Now, you'll see that I'm on the paint tab here across

18
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:02.119
the top and I've already loaded in

19
00:01:02.119 --> 00:01:05.239
my landscape material and it has two layers in it,

20
00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:06.799
grass and mud.

21
00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.599
At the moment, it is all set to grass.

22
00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:12.719
What I'm going to do is,

23
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:14.920
I'm going to change over to the mud layer,

24
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.950
and I'm just going to paint on some mud.

25
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.629
You'll see the PCG is now

26
00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:27.260
populating some stones based on that layer.

27
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.219
It's reading it into the landscape material,

28
00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:34.299
and I can design my landscape and

29
00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:39.390
my PCG just by painting it into the world.

30
00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.739
That's how we do it.

31
00:01:42.739 --> 00:01:45.659
Let's see how this was put together.

32
00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:48.450
The first thing is we need a landscape material,

33
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.010
which is set to this.

34
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.209
Here we have in our landscape material,

35
00:01:52.209 --> 00:01:55.849
important part is our landscape layer blend,

36
00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:57.930
going into our material outlet,

37
00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.390
our material attributes, and we have two.

38
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.670
We have a grass layer and we have a mud.

39
00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:10.509
This is going to dictate what material is on the land.

40
00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:16.520
Now, if we look into our PCG graph,

41
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.479
we have a very simple, straightforward PCG graph.

42
00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:22.919
We are getting the landscape data as before,

43
00:02:22.919 --> 00:02:25.840
and we're coming in and transforming the points.

44
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.879
But what is quite interesting,

45
00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:32.840
I'll see if I can get it up here as a debug object.

46
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.240
If I was to click OK,

47
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:43.039
let's have a look, you can see that at the end here,

48
00:02:43.039 --> 00:02:45.199
we have an extra attribute which is

49
00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:48.800
automatically getting added to our points attributes.

50
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.639
That is the grass and the mud layer.

51
00:02:51.639 --> 00:02:53.479
What these are is they give us

52
00:02:53.479 --> 00:02:57.360
a variable of how much that layer is painted.

53
00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.560
You can see at some points it's halfway between the two.

54
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.219
Most is over one or zero.

55
00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:08.259
That is what I am filtering on here.

56
00:03:08.259 --> 00:03:14.860
If I look at this filter attribute element,

57
00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.180
I'm looking at the attribute grass,

58
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.060
that's going to be this grass attribute here saying,

59
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.620
is it more than,

60
00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.979
and I'm doing a constant threshold of zero.

61
00:03:26.979 --> 00:03:29.099
Those are the elements that you need to change.

62
00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:30.860
I'm doing exactly the same here for the mud.

63
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.620
I'm just changing the mud over,

64
00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.020
and I've changed over what meshes we are smalling.

65
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:40.139
But it's just an example of

66
00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:44.379
how a lot of different stuff gets placed into attributes.

67
00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:46.139
As soon as there is an attribute,

68
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:52.840
you can sit there and use them and filter by them.

69
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.960
That's all you need to do.

70
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.039
Obviously, it can get vastly more complicated,

71
00:03:58.039 --> 00:04:01.159
but it just demonstrates quickly

72
00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:04.680
how you can get more data into your PCG

73
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.719
to change the way it works.
